## Android: keeping playback alive (foreground service + MediaSession)

### Why foreground services are critical on Android

Android can kill background-started processes quickly (especially after alarms, or when Android Auto starts the app) unless the app is in a foreground service state with a notification.

Bible Alarm uses foreground services to:
- survive long bootstrap/migration work
- survive media download / preparation
- keep Android Auto connected/controllable

### Foreground service coordination (single-owner rule)

Android only allows one foreground service “owner” to be active at a time for the app.

Coordinator:
- `src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/Android/Services/Media/ForegroundServiceCoordinator.cs`

Key ideas:
- coordinates ownership between:
  - MediaElement’s media controls service (notification ID 1)
  - Android Auto service (notification ID 2)
  - Alarm foreground service (notification ID 2)
- starts Android Auto foreground with placeholder metadata if real metadata isn’t ready yet (prevents kill during bootstrap)
- on alarm trigger, starts an “Alarm foreground service” immediately, then hands off to MediaElement when playback starts

Alarm entrypoint calls:
- `ForegroundServiceCoordinator.OnAlarmTriggered(context, scheduleId)` from `AlarmRingerReceiver`

### MediaSessionCompat (Android Auto + system controls)

Manager:
- `src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/Android/Services/Media/MediaSessionManager.cs`

Key behaviors:
- creates/owns a shared `MediaSessionCompat`
- updates playback state + metadata so Android Auto and system UI can control playback
- **keeps the MediaSession active even when playback is stopped** so Android Auto can discover the app when the car connects

